{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DnPEfMPhgzePduAMqQH3L6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Token normalization, destructive checks, and decision models.\"\"\"\n2\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n3\t\n4\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n5\t\"\"\"Governance Hook: parser-aware block of destructive Bash commands.\n6\t\n7\tRegistered in `.claude/settings.json` under `hooks.PreToolUse.Bash`.\n8\tRuns via the `_python-hook.sh` shim. Replaces 5 substring-matching\n9\t`Bash(...)` `if:` rules that bit us twice during Sprint 2 execution:\n10\t\n11\t- `echo \"git reset --hard\"` inside a string → falsely blocked\n12\t- A compound command containing `rm -rf` inside quotes → falsely blocked\n13\t\n14\t## Root cause of the old rules\n15\t\n16\tClaude Code's `if: \"Bash(rm -rf*)\"` does substring/prefix matching on\n17\tthe raw command string. It has no parser awareness: quoted strings\n18\tmatch, subcommands can't be targeted individually, and the rule language\n19\thas no way to express \"only when the first token is `rm`\".\n20\t\n21\t## Fix: shlex-based parser\n22\t\n23\tThis hook:\n24\t\n25\t1. Reads `tool_input.command` from the hook stdin JSON.\n26\t2. Legacy split/token rules run first; PLAN-195 adds one bounded, additive\n27\t   scanner of shell command positions and nested shell bodies.\n28\t4. Applies three rules per subcommand:\n29\t   - `rm` with both `-r` and `-f` (catches `-rf`, `-fr`, `-Rf`, `-r -f`)\n30\t   - `git reset --hard` (exact first 3 tokens)\n31\t   - `git push --force` or `git push -f` — but NOT `--force-with-lease`\n32\t5. Any match → block with a concrete remediation; infrastructure errors\n33\t   still fail-open.\n34\t\n35\t## Output contract\n36\t\n37\tWrites a single-line JSON decision to stdout:\n38\t\n39\t    {}                                      # allow\n40\t    {\"decision\":\"block\",\"reason\":\"BLOCKED: ...\"}\n41\t\n42\tPLAN-135 W2 H5 adds a THIRD shape — a corrective `updatedInput` rewrite\n43\tfor the `git push --force`/`-f` pilot pattern (single subcommand only):\n44\t\n45\t    {\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\n46\t      \"permissionDecision\":\"ask\",\n47\t      \"permissionDecisionReason\":\"REWRITTEN ... --force-with-lease ...\",\n48\t      \"updatedInput\":{\"command\":\"git push --force-with-lease\"}}}\n49\t\n50\tThe rewrite NEVER degrades a BLOCK into a silent allow: it always emits\n51\t`ask` (the permission prompt is retained, constraint (b)), and any\n52\tambiguity / compound command falls back to the legacy BLOCK\n53\t(constraint (a)). Exit code is 0 in every case — Claude Code reads the\n54\tdecision from stdout, not the exit code.\n55\t\"\"\"\n56\t\n57\t\n58\timport hashlib\n59\timport json\n60\timport re\n61\timport shlex\n62\timport sys\n63\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n64\tfrom pathlib import Path\n65\tfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional\n66\t\n67\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n68\t# PLAN-019 P0-01 / P0-02 — destructive-command bypass hardening\n69\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n70\t#\n71\t# Two coordinated fixes to the destructive-command matchers below:\n72\t#\n73\t#   P0-01: recognize long-option spellings of destructive flags.\n74\t#     Old: `rm --recursive --force dir` ALLOWED (flag-char scan skipped\n75\t#          any token starting with \"--\").\n76\t#     New: long options `--recursive`, `-r`, `--force` (and `--x=value`\n77\t#          forms) are parsed with exact-name matching.\n78\t#\n79\t#   P0-02: strip privilege-escalation prefixes and absolute/escaped paths\n80\t#     before the literal-equality check on ``tokens[0]``.\n81\t#     Old: `sudo rm -rf /`, `/bin/rm -rf /`, and `\\rm -rf /` all ALLOWED\n82\t#          because ``tokens[0] != \"rm\"`` in each case.\n83\t#     New: we normalize ``tokens`` via :func:`_normalize_command_tokens`\n84\t#          so each matcher sees the effective command basename.\n85\t#\n86\t# These declarations remain part of the Bash-safety policy. The hook facade\n87\t# loads them from this installable library module and preserves their public\n88\t# and private names for callers and tests.\n89\t\n90\t\n91\t_PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES = frozenset({\"sudo\", \"doas\", \"nocorrect\"})\n92\t\n93\t# >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (env-assignment normalization)\n94\t# A leading shell env assignment (`FOO=1 rm -rf /`, `env FOO=1 rm -rf /`)\n95\t# defeated the tokens[0] literal-equality check in every destructive matcher\n96\t# (same bypass class P0-02 closed for sudo/absolute-path spellings). Closing\n97\t# it here is a precondition for the citation gate: the gate's own structured\n98\t# channel is a leading `CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE=...` assignment, which must make\n99\t# the command MORE scrutinized, never less. `env` is folded into the\n100\t# privilege-prefix strip (its value-taking flags -u/-C/--chdir/--unset are\n101\t# consumed like sudo's -u USER).\n102\t_ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=\")\n103\t_PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES = frozenset({\"sudo\", \"doas\", \"nocorrect\", \"env\"})\n104\t_PREFIX_VALUE_FLAGS = (\"-u\", \"--user\", \"-C\", \"--chdir\", \"--unset\")\n105\t# <<< PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate END\n106\t\n107\t\n108\tdef _normalize_command_tokens(tokens: List[str]) -> List[str]:\n109\t    \"\"\"Strip privilege-escalation prefixes + normalize the command token.\n110\t\n111\t    Behavior (see P0-02 above):\n112\t\n113\t    - Drops leading ``sudo`` / ``doas`` / ``nocorrect`` tokens AND the\n114\t      flags that belong to them (``-u USER``, ``--user=USER``, ``-i``,\n115\t      ``-s``, ``-k``, ``-n``, ``-E``, etc.). Heuristic: after the prefix,\n116\t      any token starting with ``-`` is consumed as a prefix-flag; a known\n117\t      value-taking flag (``-u`` / ``--user``) additionally consumes the\n118\t      following non-flag token as its argument.\n119\t    - Replaces ``tokens[0]`` with its basename (``/bin/rm`` → ``rm``,\n120\t      ``./rm`` → ``rm``) after stripping a single leading backslash\n121\t      (shell alias-escape: ``\\\\rm`` → ``rm``).\n122\t    - PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175: additionally drops leading shell env\n123\t      assignments (``NAME=VALUE``) and the ``env`` prefix runner, so\n124\t      ``FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / ``env FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / the citation channel\n125\t      ``CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm -rf /`` all reach the same matcher\n126\t      decision as a bare ``rm -rf /``.\n127\t\n128\t    Returns a NEW list; never mutates ``tokens``. Empty input returns\n129\t    the input unchanged. Pure function (no I/O).\n130\t\n131\t    Examples\n132\t    --------\n133\t    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n134\t    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n135\t    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"/bin/rm\", \"-rf\", \"/tmp\"])\n136\t    ['rm', '-rf', '/tmp']\n137\t    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"\\\\\\\\rm\", \"-rf\", \"/tmp\"])\n138\t    ['rm', '-rf', '/tmp']\n139\t    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"-u\", \"root\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n140\t    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n141\t    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"doas\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n142\t    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n143\t    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([])\n144\t    []\n145\t    \"\"\"\n146\t    if not tokens:\n147\t        return tokens\n148\t    working = list(tokens)\n149\t    # Drop leading privilege prefixes (sudo/doas/nocorrect/env) and any flags\n150\t    # that belong to them.\n151\t    # >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (strip-loop restructure)\n152\t    # Interleaved fixed-point loop: leading NAME=VALUE env assignments and\n153\t    # privilege/env prefixes may alternate (`FOO=1 sudo rm …`,\n154\t    # `sudo env FOO=1 rm …`, `CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm …`) — keep\n155\t    # stripping until the first real command token. Pure; never raises.\n156\t    while working:\n157\t        if _ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE.match(working[0]):\n158\t            working.pop(0)  # leading shell env assignment (NAME=VALUE)\n159\t            continue\n160\t        if working[0] in _PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES:\n161\t            working.pop(0)\n162\t            # Consume prefix-owned flags: any -flag until we hit a non-flag\n163\t            # or run out. For value-taking flags (-u USER / --user=USER /\n164\t            # env's -C DIR / --chdir DIR / --unset NAME), also pop the\n165\t            # following non-flag value token.\n166\t            while working and working[0].startswith(\"-\"):\n167\t                flag = working.pop(0)\n168\t                if (flag in _PREFIX_VALUE_FLAGS\n169\t                        and working\n170\t                        and not working[0].startswith(\"-\")):\n171\t                    working.pop(0)  # consume the flag's value arg\n172\t            continue\n173\t        break\n174\t    # <<< PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate END\n175\t    if not working:\n176\t        return working\n177\t    first = working[0]\n178\t    # Strip leading backslash (alias-escape: ``\\rm`` ≡ ``rm``).\n179\t    if first.startswith(\"\\\\\"):\n180\t        first = first.lstrip(\"\\\\\")\n181\t    # Basename (\"/bin/rm\" → \"rm\", \"./rm\" → \"rm\"). Path.name is empty for\n182\t    # pure-slash strings; fall back to the pre-basename value so a weird\n183\t    # path like \"/\" doesn't crash downstream matchers.\n184\t    basename = Path(first).name if first else first\n185\t    working[0] = basename or first\n186\t    return working\n187\t\n188\t# Make the _lib package importable — hooks live in .claude/hooks/ and\n189\t# _lib is a sibling package.\n190\t_HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n191\tif str(_HOOKS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n192\t    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HOOKS_DIR))\n193\t\n194\tfrom _lib import contract as _contract  # noqa: E402\n195\tfrom _lib import credentials as _credentials  # noqa: E402\n196\tfrom _lib import git_bypass as _git_bypass  # noqa: E402\n197\tfrom _lib import adapters as _adapters  # noqa: E402\n198\t\n199\t# PLAN-155 Wave 1 (debate A1): dispatch migrated to the shared seam\n200\t# ``_adapters.resolve()`` (resolved once per invocation in ``main()``).\n201\t# The direct claude-adapter import below is RETAINED solely as a\n202\t# back-compat alias for existing in-process tests that call\n203\t# ``check_bash_safety._claude_adapter.write_decision`` directly\n204\t# (test_check_bash_safety_h5_rewrite.py); no dispatch path uses it.\n205\t# Remove when that test migrates to the seam-era surface.\n206\tfrom _lib.adapters import claude as _claude_adapter  # noqa: E402,F401\n207\ttry:\n208\t    from _lib import runtime_paths as _rp  # noqa: E402  # PLAN-182 W1 single resolver\n209\texcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover — partial upgrade: hook stays FAIL-OPEN (rail r1 P1-4)\n210\t    _rp = None  # type: ignore[assignment]\n211\t\n212\t\n213\tdef _rp_state_dir():\n214\t    \"\"\"Resolver com fallback de partial-upgrade (arquivo novo ausente).\n215\t\n216\t    Fail-open: o hook NUNCA crasha por falta do resolvedor; degrada ao\n217\t    comportamento legado com aviso em stderr.\n218\t    \"\"\"\n219\t    if _rp is not None:\n220\t        return _rp.runtime_state_dir()\n221\t    import sys as _s\n222\t    _s.stderr.write(\"# hook: _lib/runtime_paths ausente — fallback legado (partial upgrade)\\n\")\n223\t    from pathlib import Path as _P\n224\t    import os as _o\n225\t    _h = _o.environ.get(\"HOME\") or str(_P.home())\n226\t    return _P(_h) / \".claude\" / \"projects\" / \"ceo-orchestration\"  # rp-allow: partial-upgrade-fallback\n227\t\n228\ttry:  # noqa: E402\n229\t    from _lib import audit_emit as _audit_emit  # noqa: E402\n230\texcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n231\t    _audit_emit = None  # type: ignore\n232\t\n233\ttry:  # noqa: E402\n234\t    from _lib import trusted_env as _trusted_env  # noqa: E402\n235\texcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n236\t    _trusted_env = None  # type: ignore\n237\t\n238\ttry:  # noqa: E402\n239\t    from _lib import env_guard as _env_guard  # noqa: E402\n240\texcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n241\t    _env_guard = None  # type: ignore\n242\t\n243\ttry:  # noqa: E402\n244\t    from _lib import egress_taxonomy as _egress_taxonomy  # noqa: E402\n245\texcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n246\t    _egress_taxonomy = None  # type: ignore\n247\t\n248\t# PLAN-124 WS-1 — git hook-bypass guard dual-auth escape hatch (MF-E).\n249\t# Reuses the proven canonical-edit dual-auth pattern\n250\t# (check_canonical_edit.py:701-705, ADR-040-AMEND-2 §Layer-1): a non-empty\n251\t# reason/ticket env var + the literal `_ACK == \"I-ACCEPT\"` + a ticket regex.\n252\t# CRITICAL: both vars are read from the IMPORT-TIME trusted_env snapshot\n253\t# (`_lib.trusted_env.get_trusted`), NEVER live os.environ, so a late-set\n254\t# (post-anchor) value injected by a sub-agent / subprocess cannot grant the\n255\t# bypass. Both names begin with `CEO_` so they are captured by the snapshot.\n256\t_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW_VAR = \"CEO_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW\"\n257\t_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW_ACK_VAR = \"CEO_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW_ACK\"\n258\t_GIT_BYPASS_TICKET_RE = re.compile(\n259\t    r\"^(ADR-\\d{3,4}|PLAN-\\d{3})-[a-z0-9-]{3,100}$\"\n260\t)\n261\t\n262\t\n263\t# Split on top-level &&, ||, ;, | — shell control operators.\n264\t# NOTE: this is a NAIVE split that ignores quoting. A command like\n265\t# `echo \"a && b\"` will be over-split into `echo \"a` and `b\"`, both of\n266\t# which fail to tokenize cleanly under shlex. PLAN-152 error-handling-01:\n267\t# such chunks are NOT silently skipped anymore — they fall through to the\n268\t# raw-text destructive-signature rescan (`_rawscan_destructive`), because\n269\t# real bash still executes the destructive core of e.g. `rm -rf ~ \";\"`\n270\t# even though the mangled chunk defeats the token rules.\n271\t_SUBCOMMAND_SPLIT_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*(?:&&|\\|\\||[;|])\\s*\")\n272\t\n273\t\n274\t@dataclass\n275\tclass Rewrite:\n276\t    \"\"\"A corrective `updatedInput` rewrite (PLAN-135 W2 H5).\n277\t\n278\t    Carries the new command string + the before/after sha256 hashes used\n279\t    for the audit event (`bash_input_rewritten`) and a closed-enum\n280\t    `rewrite_class`. Built ONLY by :func:`_rewrite_git_push_force` and\n281\t    ONLY for the single-subcommand `git push --force`/`-f` pilot pattern.\n282\t    The single-rewriter invariant (mini-ADR-154) is structural here: at\n283\t    most ONE Rewrite is ever attached to a Decision, and the hook never\n284\t    chains a second rewrite within the same tool-call.\n285\t    \"\"\"\n286\t\n287\t    new_command: str\n288\t    before_sha256: str\n289\t    after_sha256: str\n290\t    rewrite_class: str  # closed enum (audit) — only \"git_push_force_to_lease\"\n291\t    reason: str  # human-readable; NAMES the rewrite for the permission prompt\n292\t\n293\t\n294\t@dataclass\n295\tclass Decision:\n296\t    \"\"\"Typed result of the safety check.\n297\t\n298\t    A Decision is one of three shapes:\n299\t      * allow  (allow=True, rewrite=None)               → empty `{}`\n300\t      * block  (allow=False, reason=...)                → block JSON\n301\t      * rewrite-ask (allow=True, rewrite=Rewrite(...))  → permissionDecision\n302\t        `ask` + `updatedInput` (constraint (b): the rewritten command STILL\n303\t        goes through the permission prompt; the prompt text NAMES the\n304\t        rewrite). The rewrite-ask shape is materialized in `main()` (it\n305\t        needs the adapter vendor channel), NOT in `to_json()`.\n306\t    \"\"\"\n307\t\n308\t    allow: bool\n309\t    reason: Optional[str] = None\n310\t    rewrite: Optional[\"Rewrite\"] = None\n311\t    # >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (destructive tag)\n312\t    # Destructive-command class, including PLAN-195's indirect forms. Citation\n313\t    # gating uses the tag, never reason-string matching.\n314\t    destructive: bool = False\n315\t    reason_code: Optional[str] = None\n316\t    # <<< PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate END\n317\t\n318\t    def to_json(self) -> str:\n319\t        # Allow: emit empty JSON object — Claude Code hook schema rejects\n320\t        # top-level {\"decision\":\"allow\"} (enum is \"approve\"|\"block\"; \"allow\"\n321\t        # is only valid inside hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision).\n322\t        # NOTE: to_json() is the BLOCK/ALLOW serializer only; the rewrite-ask\n323\t        # shape (updatedInput) is emitted by main() via the adapter `extra`\n324\t        # channel and never round-trips through here.\n325\t        if self.allow:\n326\t            return json.dumps({}, ensure_ascii=False)\n327\t        return json.dumps(\n328\t            {\"decision\": \"block\", \"reason\": self.reason or \"\"},\n329\t            ensure_ascii=False,\n330\t        )\n331\t\n332\t\n333\tdef _split_subcommands(command: str) -> List[str]:\n334\t    \"\"\"Split on &&, ||, ;, |. Empty/whitespace chunks are dropped.\"\"\"\n335\t    if not command or not command.strip():\n336\t        return []\n337\t    parts = _SUBCOMMAND_SPLIT_RE.split(command)\n338\t    return [p for p in (s.strip() for s in parts) if p]\n339\t\n340\t\n341\tdef _tokenize(subcommand: str) -> Optional[List[str]]:\n342\t    \"\"\"shlex.split; returns None on parse error (unbalanced quotes, etc.).\n343\t\n344\t    A parse failure is fail-OPEN for the token rules, NOT fail-safe: bash\n345\t    itself may still execute the destructive core of a chunk the naive\n346\t    subcommand splitter mangled (e.g. `rm -rf ~ \";\"` splits into an\n347\t    unbalanced-quote chunk that shlex rejects, while real bash runs\n348\t    `rm -rf ~ ';'`). Callers must treat None as \"unscanned\", not \"clean\" —\n349\t    `decide_command` routes None through the raw-text destructive-signature\n350\t    rescan (`_rawscan_destructive`, PLAN-152 error-handling-01 / debate C4).\n351\t    \"\"\"\n352\t    try:\n353\t        return shlex.split(subcommand)\n354\t    except ValueError:\n355\t        return None\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tdef _check_rm_rf(tokens: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n359\t    \"\"\"Return a block reason if tokens match `rm` with -r AND -f.\n360\t\n361\t    Post PLAN-019 P0-01 + P0-02:\n362\t\n363\t    - Privilege prefixes (``sudo``/``doas``/``nocorrect``) + their flags\n364\t      are stripped via :func:`_normalize_command_tokens`.\n365\t    - Absolute-path invocations (``/bin/rm``) and escaped-alias\n366\t      invocations (``\\\\rm``) are normalized to ``rm`` by the same helper.\n367\t    - Long options ``--recursive``, ``-r`` (alias), ``--force`` and their\n368\t      ``=value`` forms are detected alongside the original short-form\n369\t      character scan (``-rf``, ``-fr``, ``-Rf``, etc.).\n370\t    \"\"\"\n371\t    tokens = _normalize_command_tokens(tokens)\n372\t    if not tokens or tokens[0] != \"rm\":\n373\t        return None\n374\t    has_r = False\n375\t    has_f = False\n376\t    for t in tokens[1:]:\n377\t        if not t.startswith(\"-\"):\n378\t            continue  # positional argument\n379\t        if t.startswith(\"--\"):\n380\t            # Long option: match exact names (+ optional =value tail).\n381\t            # The ``=value`` form on ``--recursive``/``--force`` is NON-\n382\t            # STANDARD for POSIX ``rm`` (which treats both as boolean\n383\t            # flags). Security-first interpretation: if someone types\n384\t            # ``--recursive=<value>`` they are almost certainly probing\n385\t            # the parser; treat the equals-form on destructive flags as\n386\t            # implying BOTH ``-r`` and ``-f`` (defensive fail-closed).\n387\t            # See PLAN-019 P0-01 live-verification vector\n388\t            # ``rm --recursive=true /tmp``.\n389\t            has_eq = \"=\" in t\n390\t            name = t[2:].split(\"=\", 1)[0].lower()\n391\t            if name in (\"recursive\", \"r\"):\n392\t                has_r = True\n393\t                if has_eq:\n394\t                    has_f = True\n395\t            elif name == \"force\":\n396\t                has_f = True\n397\t                if has_eq:\n398\t                    has_r = True\n399\t            if has_r and has_f:\n400\t                break\n401\t            continue\n402\t        body = t[1:]  # strip single leading dash\n403\t        # Case-insensitive match catches -Rf, -rF, etc.\n404\t        lowered = body.lower()\n405\t        if \"r\" in lowered:\n406\t            has_r = True\n407\t        if \"f\" in lowered:\n408\t            has_f = True\n409\t        if has_r and has_f:\n410\t            break\n411\t    if has_r and has_f:\n412\t        return (\n413\t            \"BLOCKED: `rm` with -r and -f is destructive. \"\n414\t            \"Specify exact files (`rm <file>` without -r), use trash-cli, \"\n415\t            \"or run the command outside Claude Code if you really mean it.\"\n416\t        )\n417\t    return None\n418\t\n419\t\n420\tdef _check_git_reset_hard(tokens: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n421\t    \"\"\"Return a block reason if tokens start with `git reset --hard`.\n422\t\n423\t    Post PLAN-019 P0-02: tokens are normalized (``sudo git …``,\n424\t    ``/usr/bin/git …``, ``\\\\git …`` all reach the same decision).\n425\t    \"\"\"\n426\t    tokens = _normalize_command_tokens(tokens)\n427\t    if (\n428\t        len(tokens) >= 3\n429\t        and tokens[0] == \"git\"\n430\t        and tokens[1] == \"reset\"\n431\t        and tokens[2] == \"--hard\"\n432\t    ):\n433\t        return (\n434\t            \"BLOCKED: `git reset --hard` is destructive. \"\n435\t            \"Use `git stash` to save uncommitted changes, or \"\n436\t            \"`git checkout <file>` to discard specific files.\"\n437\t        )\n438\t    return None\n439\t\n440\t\n441\tdef _check_git_push_force(tokens: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n442\t    \"\"\"Return a block reason if tokens are a `git push --force` / `-f`.\n443\t\n444\t    Does NOT block `--force-with-lease`, which is the safe alternative.\n445\t\n446\t    Post PLAN-019 P0-02: tokens are normalized (``sudo git …``,\n447\t    ``/usr/bin/git …``, ``\\\\git …`` all reach the same decision).\n448\t    \"\"\"\n449\t    tokens = _normalize_command_tokens(tokens)\n450\t    if len(tokens) < 3 or tokens[0] != \"git\" or tokens[1] != \"push\":\n451\t        return None\n452\t    for t in tokens[2:]:\n453\t        if t == \"--force\" or t == \"-f\":\n454\t            return (\n455\t                \"BLOCKED: `git push --force` is destructive. \"\n456\t                \"Use `git push --force-with-lease` to avoid overwriting \"\n457\t                \"unseen commits pushed by others.\"\n458\t            )\n459\t    return None\n460\t\n461\t\n462\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n463\t# PLAN-152 error-handling-01 — shlex.ValueError bypass close (Codex R3)\n464\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n465\t#\n466\t# Closes the destructive-command bypass where the NAIVE subcommand split\n467\t# (`_split_subcommands`, a quoting-blind regex) breaks inside quotes, so\n468\t# `rm -rf ~ \";\"` splits into an unbalanced-quote chunk shlex rejects — the\n469\t# token rules never see it, yet real bash runs `rm -rf ~` (the quoted `;`\n470\t# is a literal argument).\n471\t#\n472\t# Fix history:\n473\t#   R1 (regex rescan of the raw chunk)  — Codex R2 P2#1: false-positived\n474\t#     on quoted literals like `echo \"a && rm -rf /tmp\"`.\n475\t#   R2 (`shlex.split` the whole command, segment on control-op TOKENS) —\n476\t#     Codex R3: shlex.split neither separates ADJACENT operators\n477\t#     (`true&&rm` stays one token → `rm` hides → bypass) NOR preserves\n478\t#     quoting (a quoted standalone `;` in `echo ';' rm -rf /tmp` looks\n479\t#     like a real separator → false positive).\n480\t#   R3 (this) — a QUOTE-AWARE subcommand splitter: it walks the command\n481\t#     char-by-char, honoring single/double quotes + backslash escapes (so\n482\t#     a quoted metachar is NEVER a separator) and recognizing operators\n483\t#     with NO surrounding whitespace. Each subcommand it yields has\n484\t#     balanced quotes, so the per-subcommand `shlex.split` + token rules\n485\t#     are exact. Blocks `rm -rf ~ \";\"`, `true&&rm -rf ~ ';'`,\n486\t#     `git pull && rm -rf ~ \";\"`; ALLOWs `echo \"a && rm -rf /tmp\"`,\n487\t#     `echo ';' rm -rf /tmp`. Verified against a 16-case adversarial\n488\t#     battery.\n489\t#\n490\t# A genuinely-unbalanced command (no closing quote) yields a subcommand\n491\t# that still fails shlex → skipped → ALLOW; bash would syntax-error too,\n492\t# and the `_e3` whole-command gate (a DIFFERENT tokenizer) already blocks\n493\t# the parse-rejectable class upstream (debate C4's reason this is not a\n494\t# blanket fail-closed).\n495\t#\n496\t# Kill-switch: CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN=0 reverts to the pre-PLAN-152 skip\n497\t# behavior (default-ON; read from the import-time trusted_env snapshot,\n498\t# NOT live os.environ, so a late-set value cannot disarm it mid-op).\n499\t\n500\t_RAWSCAN_DISABLE_VAR = \"CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN\"\n501\t\n502\t\n503\tdef _rawscan_enabled() -> bool:\n504\t    \"\"\"True unless CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN == \"0\" in the trusted_env snapshot.\n505\t\n506\t    Default-ON (a security tightening ships armed); the kill-switch\n507\t    exists for no-redeploy rollback (PLAN-152 §Approach). On snapshot\n508\t    unavailability it stays ON — the recheck is pure and cannot raise.\n509\t    Never raises.\n510\t    \"\"\"\n511\t    if _trusted_env is None:  # pragma: no cover — import failure → stay armed\n512\t        return True\n513\t    try:\n514\t        raw = _trusted_env.get_trusted(_RAWSCAN_DISABLE_VAR)\n515\t    except Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n516\t        return True\n517\t    return (str(raw).strip() != \"0\") if raw is not None else True\n518\t\n519\t\n520\tdef _split_subcommands_quote_aware(command: str) -> List[str]:\n521\t    \"\"\"Split on UNQUOTED &&, ||, ;, | — honoring single/double quotes and\n522\t    backslash escapes; operators need no surrounding whitespace.\n523\t\n524\t    Unlike `_split_subcommands` (a quoting-blind regex) this never splits\n525\t    on a metachar inside quotes, and unlike `shlex.split` it separates\n526\t    adjacent operators. Returns stripped, non-empty subcommand strings.\n527\t    Pure; never raises.\n528\t    \"\"\"\n529\t    parts = []  # type: List[str]\n530\t    buf = []  # type: List[str]\n531\t    i = 0\n532\t    n = len(command)\n533\t    quote = None  # type: Optional[str]\n534\t    while i < n:\n535\t        c = command[i]\n536\t        if quote is not None:\n537\t            # Inside quotes: only the matching quote closes. Inside DOUBLE\n538\t            # quotes a backslash escapes the next char (so \\\" does not\n539\t            # close); inside single quotes nothing escapes.\n540\t            if c == \"\\\\\" and quote == '\"' and i + 1 < n:\n541\t                buf.append(c)\n542\t                buf.append(command[i + 1])\n543\t                i += 2\n544\t                continue\n545\t            buf.append(c)\n546\t            if c == quote:\n547\t                quote = None\n548\t            i += 1\n549\t            continue\n550\t        if c == \"'\" or c == '\"':\n551\t            quote = c\n552\t            buf.append(c)\n553\t            i += 1\n554\t            continue\n555\t        if c == \"\\\\\" and i + 1 < n:\n556\t            buf.append(c)\n557\t            buf.append(command[i + 1])\n558\t            i += 2\n559\t            continue\n560\t        if c == \"&\" and i + 1 < n and command[i + 1] == \"&\":\n561\t            parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n562\t            buf = []\n563\t            i += 2\n564\t            continue\n565\t        if c == \"|\" and i + 1 < n and command[i + 1] == \"|\":\n566\t            parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n567\t            buf = []\n568\t            i += 2\n569\t            continue\n570\t        if c == \";\" or c == \"|\":\n571\t            parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n572\t            buf = []\n573\t            i += 1\n574\t            continue\n575\t        buf.append(c)\n576\t        i += 1\n577\t    parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n578\t    return [p for p in (s.strip() for s in parts) if p]\n579\t\n580\t\n581\tdef _recheck_whole_command(command: str) -> Optional[str]:\n582\t    \"\"\"Quote-aware recheck: block on a destructive SUBCOMMAND. Returns a\n583\t    block reason or None (allow). Pure; never raises.\n584\t\n585\t    Called ONLY when a naive-split chunk failed to tokenize (see\n586\t    `decide_command`). Re-splits the WHOLE command with the quote-aware\n587\t    splitter, then runs the token rules on each balanced-quote subcommand.\n588\t    \"\"\"\n589\t    for sub in _split_subcommands_quote_aware(command):\n590\t        toks = _tokenize(sub)\n591\t        if not toks:\n592\t            # Still unbalanced (no closing quote) → bash would syntax-\n593\t            # error too → skip (fail-safe).\n594\t            continue\n595\t        for check in (_check_rm_rf, _check_git_reset_hard, _check_git_push_force):\n596\t            reason = check(toks)\n597\t            if reason:\n598\t                # Annotate so audit + user see this came via the quote-\n599\t                # aware recheck (a chunk failed the naive tokenize).\n600\t                return reason.replace(\n601\t                    \" is destructive.\",\n602\t                    \" is destructive (re-tokenized: a quoted metachar \"\n603\t                    \"defeated the naive split — see CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN).\",\n604\t                    1,\n605\t                )\n606\t    return None\n607\t\n608\t\n609\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n610\t# PLAN-135 W2 H5 — corrective `updatedInput` rewrite (mini-ADR-154)\n611\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n612\t#\n613\t# PILOT: exactly ONE pattern — a single-subcommand `git push --force`/`-f`\n614\t# is REWRITTEN to `git push --force-with-lease` and surfaced as a\n615\t# permission-prompt (`ask`) carrying the new command via `updatedInput`,\n616\t# instead of a hard BLOCK. Constraints (NORMATIVE, debate R1 security\n617\t# must-fix; THREAT-MODEL-WORKSHEET §1; Doctrine 1 corollary):\n618\t#\n619\t#   (a) FAILURE MODE IS BLOCK. The rewrite must NEVER pass the original\n620\t#       `--force` input through on a half-applied / ambiguous rewrite. If\n621\t#       reconstruction is anything but trivially safe (compound command,\n622\t#       both flags present, an unparseable chunk, a token that does not\n623\t#       round-trip), `_rewrite_git_push_force` returns None and the caller\n624\t#       falls back to the existing BLOCK. (`fail-open §5` covers\n625\t#       hook-INFRA crashes — NOT rewrite errors.)\n626\t#   (b) STILL ASKS. The rewritten command goes through the permission\n627\t#       prompt (`permissionDecision: \"ask\"`), never a silent allow — a\n628\t#       bare `--force-with-lease` after a `git fetch` is ≈ a force-push,\n629\t#       so a human stays in the loop. `permissionDecisionReason` NAMES the\n630\t#       rewrite.\n631\t#   (c) TOKEN-LEVEL. The rewrite operates on the SAME normalized token\n632\t#       list the detector uses (`_normalize_command_tokens`), never a\n633\t#       string-level `.replace()` against the raw command (that is an\n634\t#       injection seam — `echo \"--force\" && git push -f` would mis-rewrite\n635\t#       the echo'd literal). The new command is reassembled from tokens\n636\t#       via `shlex.quote`, so quoting is re-applied safely.\n637\t#\n638\t# The single-rewriter invariant (mini-ADR-154 §1): at most ONE rewriting\n639\t# hook per tool-call; this is the only one. Downstream hooks (and the\n640\t# audit log) see the POST-rewrite input. A before/after sha256 hash PAIR\n641\t# is recorded in the `bash_input_rewritten` audit event so the audited\n642\t# command provably equals the executed command.\n643\t#\n644\t# Opt-in flag: CEO_BASH_FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE=1 ENABLES the rewrite. PILOT,\n645\t# DEFAULT-OFF (W2 H5 — until its ceremony + Codex round close): the legacy\n646\t# force-push BLOCK stays the shipped behavior, so the existing byte-identity\n647\t# fixture corpus is unchanged. Read from the import-time trusted_env snapshot\n648\t# (NOT live os.environ) so a late-set value cannot toggle the rewriter mid-op.\n649\t\n650\t_FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE_DISABLE_VAR = \"CEO_BASH_FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE\"\n651\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e86bd57-c683-4f2c-b9eb-449a331f3f20","uuid":"c90c752c-5237-4da5-9d17-ff36757a08e2","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:02:32.799Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py","content":"\"\"\"Token normalization, destructive checks, and decision models.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Governance Hook: parser-aware block of destructive Bash commands.\n\nRegistered in `.claude/settings.json` under `hooks.PreToolUse.Bash`.\nRuns via the `_python-hook.sh` shim. Replaces 5 substring-matching\n`Bash(...)` `if:` rules that bit us twice during Sprint 2 execution:\n\n- `echo \"git reset --hard\"` inside a string → falsely blocked\n- A compound command containing `rm -rf` inside quotes → falsely blocked\n\n## Root cause of the old rules\n\nClaude Code's `if: \"Bash(rm -rf*)\"` does substring/prefix matching on\nthe raw command string. It has no parser awareness: quoted strings\nmatch, subcommands can't be targeted individually, and the rule language\nhas no way to express \"only when the first token is `rm`\".\n\n## Fix: shlex-based parser\n\nThis hook:\n\n1. Reads `tool_input.command` from the hook stdin JSON.\n2. Legacy split/token rules run first; PLAN-195 adds one bounded, additive\n   scanner of shell command positions and nested shell bodies.\n4. Applies three rules per subcommand:\n   - `rm` with both `-r` and `-f` (catches `-rf`, `-fr`, `-Rf`, `-r -f`)\n   - `git reset --hard` (exact first 3 tokens)\n   - `git push --force` or `git push -f` — but NOT `--force-with-lease`\n5. Any match → block with a concrete remediation; infrastructure errors\n   still fail-open.\n\n## Output contract\n\nWrites a single-line JSON decision to stdout:\n\n    {}                                      # allow\n    {\"decision\":\"block\",\"reason\":\"BLOCKED: ...\"}\n\nPLAN-135 W2 H5 adds a THIRD shape — a corrective `updatedInput` rewrite\nfor the `git push --force`/`-f` pilot pattern (single subcommand only):\n\n    {\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\n      \"permissionDecision\":\"ask\",\n      \"permissionDecisionReason\":\"REWRITTEN ... --force-with-lease ...\",\n      \"updatedInput\":{\"command\":\"git push --force-with-lease\"}}}\n\nThe rewrite NEVER degrades a BLOCK into a silent allow: it always emits\n`ask` (the permission prompt is retained, constraint (b)), and any\nambiguity / compound command falls back to the legacy BLOCK\n(constraint (a)). Exit code is 0 in every case — Claude Code reads the\ndecision from stdout, not the exit code.\n\"\"\"\n\n\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport re\nimport shlex\nimport sys\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# PLAN-019 P0-01 / P0-02 — destructive-command bypass hardening\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#\n# Two coordinated fixes to the destructive-command matchers below:\n#\n#   P0-01: recognize long-option spellings of destructive flags.\n#     Old: `rm --recursive --force dir` ALLOWED (flag-char scan skipped\n#          any token starting with \"--\").\n#     New: long options `--recursive`, `-r`, `--force` (and `--x=value`\n#          forms) are parsed with exact-name matching.\n#\n#   P0-02: strip privilege-escalation prefixes and absolute/escaped paths\n#     before the literal-equality check on ``tokens[0]``.\n#     Old: `sudo rm -rf /`, `/bin/rm -rf /`, and `\\rm -rf /` all ALLOWED\n#          because ``tokens[0] != \"rm\"`` in each case.\n#     New: we normalize ``tokens`` via :func:`_normalize_command_tokens`\n#          so each matcher sees the effective command basename.\n#\n# These declarations remain part of the Bash-safety policy. The hook facade\n# loads them from this installable library module and preserves their public\n# and private names for callers and tests.\n\n\n_PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES = frozenset({\"sudo\", \"doas\", \"nocorrect\"})\n\n# >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (env-assignment normalization)\n# A leading shell env assignment (`FOO=1 rm -rf /`, `env FOO=1 rm -rf /`)\n# defeated the tokens[0] literal-equality check in every destructive matcher\n# (same bypass class P0-02 closed for sudo/absolute-path spellings). Closing\n# it here is a precondition for the citation gate: the gate's own structured\n# channel is a leading `CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE=...` assignment, which must make\n# the command MORE scrutinized, never less. `env` is folded into the\n# privilege-prefix strip (its value-taking flags -u/-C/--chdir/--unset are\n# consumed like sudo's -u USER).\n_ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=\")\n_PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES = frozenset({\"sudo\", \"doas\", \"nocorrect\", \"env\"})\n_PREFIX_VALUE_FLAGS = (\"-u\", \"--user\", \"-C\", \"--chdir\", \"--unset\")\n# <<< PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate END\n\n\ndef _normalize_command_tokens(tokens: List[str]) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Strip privilege-escalation prefixes + normalize the command token.\n\n    Behavior (see P0-02 above):\n\n    - Drops leading ``sudo`` / ``doas`` / ``nocorrect`` tokens AND the\n      flags that belong to them (``-u USER``, ``--user=USER``, ``-i``,\n      ``-s``, ``-k``, ``-n``, ``-E``, etc.). Heuristic: after the prefix,\n      any token starting with ``-`` is consumed as a prefix-flag; a known\n      value-taking flag (``-u`` / ``--user``) additionally consumes the\n      following non-flag token as its argument.\n    - Replaces ``tokens[0]`` with its basename (``/bin/rm`` → ``rm``,\n      ``./rm`` → ``rm``) after stripping a single leading backslash\n      (shell alias-escape: ``\\\\rm`` → ``rm``).\n    - PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175: additionally drops leading shell env\n      assignments (``NAME=VALUE``) and the ``env`` prefix runner, so\n      ``FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / ``env FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / the citation channel\n      ``CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm -rf /`` all reach the same matcher\n      decision as a bare ``rm -rf /``.\n\n    Returns a NEW list; never mutates ``tokens``. Empty input returns\n    the input unchanged. Pure function (no I/O).\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"/bin/rm\", \"-rf\", \"/tmp\"])\n    ['rm', '-rf', '/tmp']\n    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"\\\\\\\\rm\", \"-rf\", \"/tmp\"])\n    ['rm', '-rf', '/tmp']\n    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"-u\", \"root\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"doas\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([])\n    []\n    \"\"\"\n    if not tokens:\n        return tokens\n    working = list(tokens)\n    # Drop leading privilege prefixes (sudo/doas/nocorrect/env) and any flags\n    # that belong to them.\n    # >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (strip-loop restructure)\n    # Interleaved fixed-point loop: leading NAME=VALUE env assignments and\n    # privilege/env prefixes may alternate (`FOO=1 sudo rm …`,\n    # `sudo env FOO=1 rm …`, `CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm …`) — keep\n    # stripping until the first real command token. Pure; never raises.\n    while working:\n        if _ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE.match(working[0]):\n            working.pop(0)  # leading shell env assignment (NAME=VALUE)\n            continue\n        if working[0] in _PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES:\n            working.pop(0)\n            # Consume prefix-owned flags: any -flag until we hit a non-flag\n            # or run out. For value-taking flags (-u USER / --user=USER /\n            # env's -C DIR / --chdir DIR / --unset NAME), also pop the\n            # following non-flag value token.\n            while working and working[0].startswith(\"-\"):\n                flag = working.pop(0)\n                if (flag in _PREFIX_VALUE_FLAGS\n                        and working\n                        and not working[0].startswith(\"-\")):\n                    working.pop(0)  # consume the flag's value arg\n            continue\n        break\n    # <<< PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate END\n    if not working:\n        return working\n    first = working[0]\n    # Strip leading backslash (alias-escape: ``\\rm`` ≡ ``rm``).\n    if first.startswith(\"\\\\\"):\n        first = first.lstrip(\"\\\\\")\n    # Basename (\"/bin/rm\" → \"rm\", \"./rm\" → \"rm\"). Path.name is empty for\n    # pure-slash strings; fall back to the pre-basename value so a weird\n    # path like \"/\" doesn't crash downstream matchers.\n    basename = Path(first).name if first else first\n    working[0] = basename or first\n    return working\n\n# Make the _lib package importable — hooks live in .claude/hooks/ and\n# _lib is a sibling package.\n_HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nif str(_HOOKS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HOOKS_DIR))\n\nfrom _lib import contract as _contract  # noqa: E402\nfrom _lib import credentials as _credentials  # noqa: E402\nfrom _lib import git_bypass as _git_bypass  # noqa: E402\nfrom _lib import adapters as _adapters  # noqa: E402\n\n# PLAN-155 Wave 1 (debate A1): dispatch migrated to the shared seam\n# ``_adapters.resolve()`` (resolved once per invocation in ``main()``).\n# The direct claude-adapter import below is RETAINED solely as a\n# back-compat alias for existing in-process tests that call\n# ``check_bash_safety._claude_adapter.write_decision`` directly\n# (test_check_bash_safety_h5_rewrite.py); no dispatch path uses it.\n# Remove when that test migrates to the seam-era surface.\nfrom _lib.adapters import claude as _claude_adapter  # noqa: E402,F401\ntry:\n    from _lib import runtime_paths as _rp  # noqa: E402  # PLAN-182 W1 single resolver\nexcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover — partial upgrade: hook stays FAIL-OPEN (rail r1 P1-4)\n    _rp = None  # type: ignore[assignment]\n\n\ndef _rp_state_dir():\n    \"\"\"Resolver com fallback de partial-upgrade (arquivo novo ausente).\n\n    Fail-open: o hook NUNCA crasha por falta do resolvedor; degrada ao\n    comportamento legado com aviso em stderr.\n    \"\"\"\n    if _rp is not None:\n        return _rp.runtime_state_dir()\n    import sys as _s\n    _s.stderr.write(\"# hook: _lib/runtime_paths ausente — fallback legado (partial upgrade)\\n\")\n    from pathlib import Path as _P\n    import os as _o\n    _h = _o.environ.get(\"HOME\") or str(_P.home())\n    return _P(_h) / \".claude\" / \"projects\" / \"ceo-orchestration\"  # rp-allow: partial-upgrade-fallback\n\ntry:  # noqa: E402\n    from _lib import audit_emit as _audit_emit  # noqa: E402\nexcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n    _audit_emit = None  # type: ignore\n\ntry:  # noqa: E402\n    from _lib import trusted_env as _trusted_env  # noqa: E402\nexcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n    _trusted_env = None  # type: ignore\n\ntry:  # noqa: E402\n    from _lib import env_guard as _env_guard  # noqa: E402\nexcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n    _env_guard = None  # type: ignore\n\ntry:  # noqa: E402\n    from _lib import egress_taxonomy as _egress_taxonomy  # noqa: E402\nexcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n    _egress_taxonomy = None  # type: ignore\n\n# PLAN-124 WS-1 — git hook-bypass guard dual-auth escape hatch (MF-E).\n# Reuses the proven canonical-edit dual-auth pattern\n# (check_canonical_edit.py:701-705, ADR-040-AMEND-2 §Layer-1): a non-empty\n# reason/ticket env var + the literal `_ACK == \"I-ACCEPT\"` + a ticket regex.\n# CRITICAL: both vars are read from the IMPORT-TIME trusted_env snapshot\n# (`_lib.trusted_env.get_trusted`), NEVER live os.environ, so a late-set\n# (post-anchor) value injected by a sub-agent / subprocess cannot grant the\n# bypass. Both names begin with `CEO_` so they are captured by the snapshot.\n_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW_VAR = \"CEO_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW\"\n_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW_ACK_VAR = \"CEO_GIT_BYPASS_ALLOW_ACK\"\n_GIT_BYPASS_TICKET_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"^(ADR-\\d{3,4}|PLAN-\\d{3})-[a-z0-9-]{3,100}$\"\n)\n\n\n# Split on top-level &&, ||, ;, | — shell control operators.\n# NOTE: this is a NAIVE split that ignores quoting. A command like\n# `echo \"a && b\"` will be over-split into `echo \"a` and `b\"`, both of\n# which fail to tokenize cleanly under shlex. PLAN-152 error-handling-01:\n# such chunks are NOT silently skipped anymore — they fall through to the\n# raw-text destructive-signature rescan (`_rawscan_destructive`), because\n# real bash still executes the destructive core of e.g. `rm -rf ~ \";\"`\n# even though the mangled chunk defeats the token rules.\n_SUBCOMMAND_SPLIT_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*(?:&&|\\|\\||[;|])\\s*\")\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Rewrite:\n    \"\"\"A corrective `updatedInput` rewrite (PLAN-135 W2 H5).\n\n    Carries the new command string + the before/after sha256 hashes used\n    for the audit event (`bash_input_rewritten`) and a closed-enum\n    `rewrite_class`. Built ONLY by :func:`_rewrite_git_push_force` and\n    ONLY for the single-subcommand `git push --force`/`-f` pilot pattern.\n    The single-rewriter invariant (mini-ADR-154) is structural here: at\n    most ONE Rewrite is ever attached to a Decision, and the hook never\n    chains a second rewrite within the same tool-call.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    new_command: str\n    before_sha256: str\n    after_sha256: str\n    rewrite_class: str  # closed enum (audit) — only \"git_push_force_to_lease\"\n    reason: str  # human-readable; NAMES the rewrite for the permission prompt\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Decision:\n    \"\"\"Typed result of the safety check.\n\n    A Decision is one of three shapes:\n      * allow  (allow=True, rewrite=None)               → empty `{}`\n      * block  (allow=False, reason=...)                → block JSON\n      * rewrite-ask (allow=True, rewrite=Rewrite(...))  → permissionDecision\n        `ask` + `updatedInput` (constraint (b): the rewritten command STILL\n        goes through the permission prompt; the prompt text NAMES the\n        rewrite). The rewrite-ask shape is materialized in `main()` (it\n        needs the adapter vendor channel), NOT in `to_json()`.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    allow: bool\n    reason: Optional[str] = None\n    rewrite: Optional[\"Rewrite\"] = None\n    # >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (destructive tag)\n    # Destructive-command class, including PLAN-195's indirect forms. Citation\n    # gating uses the tag, never reason-string matching.\n    destructive: bool = False\n    reason_code: Optional[str] = None\n    # <<< PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate END\n\n    def to_json(self) -> str:\n        # Allow: emit empty JSON object — Claude Code hook schema rejects\n        # top-level {\"decision\":\"allow\"} (enum is \"approve\"|\"block\"; \"allow\"\n        # is only valid inside hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision).\n        # NOTE: to_json() is the BLOCK/ALLOW serializer only; the rewrite-ask\n        # shape (updatedInput) is emitted by main() via the adapter `extra`\n        # channel and never round-trips through here.\n        if self.allow:\n            return json.dumps({}, ensure_ascii=False)\n        return json.dumps(\n            {\"decision\": \"block\", \"reason\": self.reason or \"\"},\n            ensure_ascii=False,\n        )\n\n\ndef _split_subcommands(command: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Split on &&, ||, ;, |. Empty/whitespace chunks are dropped.\"\"\"\n    if not command or not command.strip():\n        return []\n    parts = _SUBCOMMAND_SPLIT_RE.split(command)\n    return [p for p in (s.strip() for s in parts) if p]\n\n\ndef _tokenize(subcommand: str) -> Optional[List[str]]:\n    \"\"\"shlex.split; returns None on parse error (unbalanced quotes, etc.).\n\n    A parse failure is fail-OPEN for the token rules, NOT fail-safe: bash\n    itself may still execute the destructive core of a chunk the naive\n    subcommand splitter mangled (e.g. `rm -rf ~ \";\"` splits into an\n    unbalanced-quote chunk that shlex rejects, while real bash runs\n    `rm -rf ~ ';'`). Callers must treat None as \"unscanned\", not \"clean\" —\n    `decide_command` routes None through the raw-text destructive-signature\n    rescan (`_rawscan_destructive`, PLAN-152 error-handling-01 / debate C4).\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        return shlex.split(subcommand)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n\n\ndef _check_rm_rf(tokens: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Return a block reason if tokens match `rm` with -r AND -f.\n\n    Post PLAN-019 P0-01 + P0-02:\n\n    - Privilege prefixes (``sudo``/``doas``/``nocorrect``) + their flags\n      are stripped via :func:`_normalize_command_tokens`.\n    - Absolute-path invocations (``/bin/rm``) and escaped-alias\n      invocations (``\\\\rm``) are normalized to ``rm`` by the same helper.\n    - Long options ``--recursive``, ``-r`` (alias), ``--force`` and their\n      ``=value`` forms are detected alongside the original short-form\n      character scan (``-rf``, ``-fr``, ``-Rf``, etc.).\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = _normalize_command_tokens(tokens)\n    if not tokens or tokens[0] != \"rm\":\n        return None\n    has_r = False\n    has_f = False\n    for t in tokens[1:]:\n        if not t.startswith(\"-\"):\n            continue  # positional argument\n        if t.startswith(\"--\"):\n            # Long option: match exact names (+ optional =value tail).\n            # The ``=value`` form on ``--recursive``/``--force`` is NON-\n            # STANDARD for POSIX ``rm`` (which treats both as boolean\n            # flags). Security-first interpretation: if someone types\n            # ``--recursive=<value>`` they are almost certainly probing\n            # the parser; treat the equals-form on destructive flags as\n            # implying BOTH ``-r`` and ``-f`` (defensive fail-closed).\n            # See PLAN-019 P0-01 live-verification vector\n            # ``rm --recursive=true /tmp``.\n            has_eq = \"=\" in t\n            name = t[2:].split(\"=\", 1)[0].lower()\n            if name in (\"recursive\", \"r\"):\n                has_r = True\n                if has_eq:\n                    has_f = True\n            elif name == \"force\":\n                has_f = True\n                if has_eq:\n                    has_r = True\n            if has_r and has_f:\n                break\n            continue\n        body = t[1:]  # strip single leading dash\n        # Case-insensitive match catches -Rf, -rF, etc.\n        lowered = body.lower()\n        if \"r\" in lowered:\n            has_r = True\n        if \"f\" in lowered:\n            has_f = True\n        if has_r and has_f:\n            break\n    if has_r and has_f:\n        return (\n            \"BLOCKED: `rm` with -r and -f is destructive. \"\n            \"Specify exact files (`rm <file>` without -r), use trash-cli, \"\n            \"or run the command outside Claude Code if you really mean it.\"\n        )\n    return None\n\n\ndef _check_git_reset_hard(tokens: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Return a block reason if tokens start with `git reset --hard`.\n\n    Post PLAN-019 P0-02: tokens are normalized (``sudo git …``,\n    ``/usr/bin/git …``, ``\\\\git …`` all reach the same decision).\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = _normalize_command_tokens(tokens)\n    if (\n        len(tokens) >= 3\n        and tokens[0] == \"git\"\n        and tokens[1] == \"reset\"\n        and tokens[2] == \"--hard\"\n    ):\n        return (\n            \"BLOCKED: `git reset --hard` is destructive. \"\n            \"Use `git stash` to save uncommitted changes, or \"\n            \"`git checkout <file>` to discard specific files.\"\n        )\n    return None\n\n\ndef _check_git_push_force(tokens: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Return a block reason if tokens are a `git push --force` / `-f`.\n\n    Does NOT block `--force-with-lease`, which is the safe alternative.\n\n    Post PLAN-019 P0-02: tokens are normalized (``sudo git …``,\n    ``/usr/bin/git …``, ``\\\\git …`` all reach the same decision).\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = _normalize_command_tokens(tokens)\n    if len(tokens) < 3 or tokens[0] != \"git\" or tokens[1] != \"push\":\n        return None\n    for t in tokens[2:]:\n        if t == \"--force\" or t == \"-f\":\n            return (\n                \"BLOCKED: `git push --force` is destructive. \"\n                \"Use `git push --force-with-lease` to avoid overwriting \"\n                \"unseen commits pushed by others.\"\n            )\n    return None\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# PLAN-152 error-handling-01 — shlex.ValueError bypass close (Codex R3)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#\n# Closes the destructive-command bypass where the NAIVE subcommand split\n# (`_split_subcommands`, a quoting-blind regex) breaks inside quotes, so\n# `rm -rf ~ \";\"` splits into an unbalanced-quote chunk shlex rejects — the\n# token rules never see it, yet real bash runs `rm -rf ~` (the quoted `;`\n# is a literal argument).\n#\n# Fix history:\n#   R1 (regex rescan of the raw chunk)  — Codex R2 P2#1: false-positived\n#     on quoted literals like `echo \"a && rm -rf /tmp\"`.\n#   R2 (`shlex.split` the whole command, segment on control-op TOKENS) —\n#     Codex R3: shlex.split neither separates ADJACENT operators\n#     (`true&&rm` stays one token → `rm` hides → bypass) NOR preserves\n#     quoting (a quoted standalone `;` in `echo ';' rm -rf /tmp` looks\n#     like a real separator → false positive).\n#   R3 (this) — a QUOTE-AWARE subcommand splitter: it walks the command\n#     char-by-char, honoring single/double quotes + backslash escapes (so\n#     a quoted metachar is NEVER a separator) and recognizing operators\n#     with NO surrounding whitespace. Each subcommand it yields has\n#     balanced quotes, so the per-subcommand `shlex.split` + token rules\n#     are exact. Blocks `rm -rf ~ \";\"`, `true&&rm -rf ~ ';'`,\n#     `git pull && rm -rf ~ \";\"`; ALLOWs `echo \"a && rm -rf /tmp\"`,\n#     `echo ';' rm -rf /tmp`. Verified against a 16-case adversarial\n#     battery.\n#\n# A genuinely-unbalanced command (no closing quote) yields a subcommand\n# that still fails shlex → skipped → ALLOW; bash would syntax-error too,\n# and the `_e3` whole-command gate (a DIFFERENT tokenizer) already blocks\n# the parse-rejectable class upstream (debate C4's reason this is not a\n# blanket fail-closed).\n#\n# Kill-switch: CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN=0 reverts to the pre-PLAN-152 skip\n# behavior (default-ON; read from the import-time trusted_env snapshot,\n# NOT live os.environ, so a late-set value cannot disarm it mid-op).\n\n_RAWSCAN_DISABLE_VAR = \"CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN\"\n\n\ndef _rawscan_enabled() -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True unless CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN == \"0\" in the trusted_env snapshot.\n\n    Default-ON (a security tightening ships armed); the kill-switch\n    exists for no-redeploy rollback (PLAN-152 §Approach). On snapshot\n    unavailability it stays ON — the recheck is pure and cannot raise.\n    Never raises.\n    \"\"\"\n    if _trusted_env is None:  # pragma: no cover — import failure → stay armed\n        return True\n    try:\n        raw = _trusted_env.get_trusted(_RAWSCAN_DISABLE_VAR)\n    except Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n        return True\n    return (str(raw).strip() != \"0\") if raw is not None else True\n\n\ndef _split_subcommands_quote_aware(command: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Split on UNQUOTED &&, ||, ;, | — honoring single/double quotes and\n    backslash escapes; operators need no surrounding whitespace.\n\n    Unlike `_split_subcommands` (a quoting-blind regex) this never splits\n    on a metachar inside quotes, and unlike `shlex.split` it separates\n    adjacent operators. Returns stripped, non-empty subcommand strings.\n    Pure; never raises.\n    \"\"\"\n    parts = []  # type: List[str]\n    buf = []  # type: List[str]\n    i = 0\n    n = len(command)\n    quote = None  # type: Optional[str]\n    while i < n:\n        c = command[i]\n        if quote is not None:\n            # Inside quotes: only the matching quote closes. Inside DOUBLE\n            # quotes a backslash escapes the next char (so \\\" does not\n            # close); inside single quotes nothing escapes.\n            if c == \"\\\\\" and quote == '\"' and i + 1 < n:\n                buf.append(c)\n                buf.append(command[i + 1])\n                i += 2\n                continue\n            buf.append(c)\n            if c == quote:\n                quote = None\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if c == \"'\" or c == '\"':\n            quote = c\n            buf.append(c)\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if c == \"\\\\\" and i + 1 < n:\n            buf.append(c)\n            buf.append(command[i + 1])\n            i += 2\n            continue\n        if c == \"&\" and i + 1 < n and command[i + 1] == \"&\":\n            parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n            buf = []\n            i += 2\n            continue\n        if c == \"|\" and i + 1 < n and command[i + 1] == \"|\":\n            parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n            buf = []\n            i += 2\n            continue\n        if c == \";\" or c == \"|\":\n            parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n            buf = []\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        buf.append(c)\n        i += 1\n    parts.append(\"\".join(buf))\n    return [p for p in (s.strip() for s in parts) if p]\n\n\ndef _recheck_whole_command(command: str) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Quote-aware recheck: block on a destructive SUBCOMMAND. Returns a\n    block reason or None (allow). Pure; never raises.\n\n    Called ONLY when a naive-split chunk failed to tokenize (see\n    `decide_command`). Re-splits the WHOLE command with the quote-aware\n    splitter, then runs the token rules on each balanced-quote subcommand.\n    \"\"\"\n    for sub in _split_subcommands_quote_aware(command):\n        toks = _tokenize(sub)\n        if not toks:\n            # Still unbalanced (no closing quote) → bash would syntax-\n            # error too → skip (fail-safe).\n            continue\n        for check in (_check_rm_rf, _check_git_reset_hard, _check_git_push_force):\n            reason = check(toks)\n            if reason:\n                # Annotate so audit + user see this came via the quote-\n                # aware recheck (a chunk failed the naive tokenize).\n                return reason.replace(\n                    \" is destructive.\",\n                    \" is destructive (re-tokenized: a quoted metachar \"\n                    \"defeated the naive split — see CEO_BASH_RAWSCAN).\",\n                    1,\n                )\n    return None\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# PLAN-135 W2 H5 — corrective `updatedInput` rewrite (mini-ADR-154)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#\n# PILOT: exactly ONE pattern — a single-subcommand `git push --force`/`-f`\n# is REWRITTEN to `git push --force-with-lease` and surfaced as a\n# permission-prompt (`ask`) carrying the new command via `updatedInput`,\n# instead of a hard BLOCK. Constraints (NORMATIVE, debate R1 security\n# must-fix; THREAT-MODEL-WORKSHEET §1; Doctrine 1 corollary):\n#\n#   (a) FAILURE MODE IS BLOCK. The rewrite must NEVER pass the original\n#       `--force` input through on a half-applied / ambiguous rewrite. If\n#       reconstruction is anything but trivially safe (compound command,\n#       both flags present, an unparseable chunk, a token that does not\n#       round-trip), `_rewrite_git_push_force` returns None and the caller\n#       falls back to the existing BLOCK. (`fail-open §5` covers\n#       hook-INFRA crashes — NOT rewrite errors.)\n#   (b) STILL ASKS. The rewritten command goes through the permission\n#       prompt (`permissionDecision: \"ask\"`), never a silent allow — a\n#       bare `--force-with-lease` after a `git fetch` is ≈ a force-push,\n#       so a human stays in the loop. `permissionDecisionReason` NAMES the\n#       rewrite.\n#   (c) TOKEN-LEVEL. The rewrite operates on the SAME normalized token\n#       list the detector uses (`_normalize_command_tokens`), never a\n#       string-level `.replace()` against the raw command (that is an\n#       injection seam — `echo \"--force\" && git push -f` would mis-rewrite\n#       the echo'd literal). The new command is reassembled from tokens\n#       via `shlex.quote`, so quoting is re-applied safely.\n#\n# The single-rewriter invariant (mini-ADR-154 §1): at most ONE rewriting\n# hook per tool-call; this is the only one. Downstream hooks (and the\n# audit log) see the POST-rewrite input. A before/after sha256 hash PAIR\n# is recorded in the `bash_input_rewritten` audit event so the audited\n# command provably equals the executed command.\n#\n# Opt-in flag: CEO_BASH_FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE=1 ENABLES the rewrite. PILOT,\n# DEFAULT-OFF (W2 H5 — until its ceremony + Codex round close): the legacy\n# force-push BLOCK stays the shipped behavior, so the existing byte-identity\n# fixture corpus is unchanged. Read from the import-time trusted_env snapshot\n# (NOT live os.environ) so a late-set value cannot toggle the rewriter mid-op.\n\n_FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE_DISABLE_VAR = \"CEO_BASH_FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE\"\n","numLines":651,"startLine":1,"totalLines":651}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DnPEfMPhgzePduAMqQH3L6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
